{"exp": "ml_breakout_quality_filter", "sym": "NAS", "block": "research", "window": "07:00-11:00 NY", "model": "LightGBM classifier, 32 causal features", "cv": "purged 6-fold, 32-bar embargo", "hypothesis": "ML can rank Donchian breakout quality well enough to filter the weak ones and clear costs", "n_trades": 4245, "auc_oof": 0.5785, "permutation_null_auc": 0.4975, "permutation_sd": 0.0084, "permutation_z": 9.7, "seed_sd": 0.001, "auc_no_time_features": 0.551, "best_filter": "top decile of out-of-fold score", "filter_n": 339, "filter_exp": 0.15, "matched_control_p": 0.08, "random_filter_p": 0.02, "breakeven_cost_multiple": 1.05, "subperiod_exp": [-1.74, 0.29, 1.89], "decision": "REJECTED", "reason": "Ranking skill is real and strongly validated (z=+9.70 against a permutation null, seed sd 0.0010), but worth only +0.15 pts/trade against a 2.50 pt round turn. Break-even cost multiple 1.05, so a 5% cost increase kills it. Fails the matched control at p=0.080. P&L is concentrated in the final third of the research block (-1.74/+0.29/+1.89), the one-lucky-window signature.", "learned": "Statistically significant predictive skill and an economically usable edge are different things. The permutation test is what makes this claim safe to state: without it, AUC 0.578 would have looked like either a triumph or a leak, and there would have been no way to tell.", "id": "E0003"}
{"exp": "entry_mechanic_stop_vs_market", "sym": "NAS", "block": "research", "window": "07:00-11:00 NY", "n_configs": 28, "hypothesis": "The authentic Donchian mechanic - a resting stop order AT the channel level - beats entering at the next bar's open after a close beyond it. CLAUDE.md calls the entry mechanic the biggest lever found on this branch.", "method": "paired: identical triggers, identical geometry, identical exit walk; only the fill differs. Needs no control. Corrects an earlier run that scored the stop-entry book against a next-open matched control, which was mismatched to the mechanic.", "fill_improvement_pts": [2.21, 2.45, 2.57], "paired_diff_by_stopmult": {"n10": [1.33, 1.47, 2.36], "n20": [-1.63, 0.88, 2.14], "n40": [-4.07, -1.71, 0.96]}, "mean_paired_diff": 0.19, "sig_better": 4, "sig_worse": 3, "best_cell": "n_entry=10, stop 2.0 ATR, targ 2.0 ATR, exp -0.78 pts/trade", "decision": "REAL MECHANISM, DOES NOT RESCUE THE FAMILY", "reason": "The stop order genuinely fills 2.2-2.6 points better than the next open, which is the size of the whole round turn. But that improvement is only retained when the stop is wide: the paired difference is monotone increasing in stop multiple and monotone decreasing in channel length, from -4.07 (n=40, stop 1.5) to +2.36 (n=10, stop 2.5). Entering earlier puts you in the noise the confirmation bar would have filtered, so a tight stop gives back more than the fill gained. Best configuration still loses 0.78 pts/trade.", "learned": "A better fill is not the same as a better trade. The mechanic interacts with the stop, and reporting either one alone would have been misleading in opposite directions.", "id": "E0004"}
